{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 16, :comments ["<b>And he shall remove its crop with its contents</b> — the place of the excrement, that is, the זפק (crop). <b>With its contents</b> — together with the entrails. And נוצה means something repulsive, as in 'כי נצו גם נעו' (Lamentations 4:15), and this is how Onkelos renders it (Onkelos on Leviticus 1:16): 'באכלה' (with its contents). And this is the midrash of Abba Yosei ben Hanan (Torat Kohanim, parasha 7:9), who said: he takes the קרקבן (gizzard) along with it. But our Rabbis said (Zevahim 65a): he cuts the זפק with a knife in the shape of an opening and takes it together with the נוצה — this is the language of Rashi (Rashi on Leviticus 1:16). And this is not correct, for נוצה nowhere means anything but actual down-feathers, and so 'כי נצו גם נעו' means that they grew wings to fly from their place into exile, and they wander there without finding rest. And similar to it is 'כי נצא תצא' (Jeremiah 48:9). And the translator of the Yerushalmi rendered it: 'ארי יתקוטטו אף יטלטלו' — he derived it from 'כי ינצו אנשים' (Deuteronomy 25:11), meaning that they are hostile among all the peoples and wander away from them and will no longer dwell among them, and he explained well. But the meaning of נוצה as feathers, as the Rav claims, will not be found.\n\nAnd the midrash of Abba Yosei, who required taking the קרקבן as well along with it, is because the זפק and the esophagus together with the קרקבן are the organs that process all the food, included within the meaning of מראתו (its crop), since it is in the קרקבן that the excrement is formed. And Abba Yosei did not disagree at all with what the first Tanna said, that he takes it with the נוצה, for behold we learned in the Mishnah in tractate Zevahim, chapter six (Zevahim 54b): he comes to the body and removes the מראה and the נוצה and the entrails that go out with it and casts them onto the ash-heap — and this Mishnah is the Mishnah of Abba Yosei, and it teaches נוצה.\n\nAnd the opinion of Onkelos is like the opinion of the Sages: he removes only the זפק with its נוצה, along with the food that has entered it, which is the מראה. And since the priest grasps the זפק and through it removes the מראה, he said 'ית זפקיה באכלה', as though the contents are in the crop, for 'אכלה' translates 'מראתו' and 'זפקיה' translates 'נוצתה', since Scripture says that he shall remove the מראה — which is the food — בנוצתה that is upon it, meaning he takes the זפק with its skin and the נוצה upon it. And thus Onkelos said (Exodus 23:21): 'ארי בשמי מימרה', and in Hebrew it would mean 'ארי שמי במימרה', but he inverted his translation for a purpose known to him. And he translated 'ואת שתי קצות שתי העבתות' (Exodus 28:25) likewise with inversion: 'וית תרתין גדילן דעל תרין סטרוהי', and so there are many."], :book :leviticus, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 1, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T03:49:48.679362Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}